# Fictional EP Tracklist

## Track 1: Side B Starts Before the Elevator Stops

**Story & Context:** At 112 BPM, Marcus opens with a two-bar electric-bass line built from tonic, sixth, octave, chromatic lower neighbor, and flat seventh while Min holds a clipped kick-snare pocket with small handclaps. Carla sustains a warm minor-7-to-dominant-9 polysynth movement with slow filter motion. Wenyi does not play melody. She enters with two Skin-Backbeats against the clap, then inserts a muted dead-string rake on the final sixteenth of beat four. The defining hook is a Slide Pickup: one short upward scoop into the ninth of the next chord, released just before Marcus lands the root.

The chorus adds a soft bilingual vocal hook shared by Carla and Xinyi, while Wenyi alternates low open-fifth dyads with dead strokes. During the bridge, Marcus holds a long tonic and Wenyi becomes more active, playing a syncopated three-note pentatonic figure with tiny fretless scoops. When the bass resumes motion, she immediately simplifies again. The track demonstrates Bass-Hand Exchange without any breakdown or solo section.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Interior of an older mirrored elevator opening onto a dim record-pressing corridor, one blank cream sleeve tucked under a person’s arm, green floor indicator showing B while the doors are still moving, amber inspection lamps reflected in brushed metal. Sparse lyric fragments: “Side B first,” “doors still moving,” “don’t wait downstairs,” “电梯还没停 / the elevator hasn’t stopped.”

## Track 2: Fingerprints on the Lacquer Master

**Story & Context:** At 118 BPM, this is the EP’s tightest dance-floor track. Min uses a dry machine-assisted kick, layered clap, sixteenth hats with strong velocity variation, and short rototom answers every fourth bar. Marcus plays shorter notes than anywhere else on the record, leaving pronounced gaps after beats one and three. Wenyi fills those gaps with Dead-String Pocket material—nail clicks, damped two-string scratches, body taps, and clipped octave plucks—so the sanxian becomes almost entirely percussive for the first sixteen bars.

Carla’s Velvet Chassis uses major-7 and add9 voicings voiced high enough to avoid the sanxian. The second verse introduces a simple sung melody, but Wenyi remains in the rhythm section rather than answering vocally. Only in the final third does she play a four-note melodic cell, each note approached by a microscopic downward slide. Xinyi punctuates phrase endings with low bass-clarinet pops and one breath-heavy descending minor third.

The lyrical idea concerns leaving marks on something supposedly meant to remain pristine: rehearsal mistakes, fingerprints on masters, handwritten labels, human timing inside systems designed for repeatability.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Extreme close-up of a black lacquer master disc under an amber inspection lamp, visible fingerprints crossing the glossy surface, a jade-green wax pencil and white cotton glove resting nearby, shallow depth of field, industrial realism. Sparse lyric fragments: “fingerprints stay,” “master copy,” “don’t polish me out,” “留下来 / leave it there.”

## Track 3: Warm Motor After Closing Time

**Story & Context:** The closer drops to 104 BPM. It begins with Carla alone on a soft major-6 polysynth chord while an old turntable motor hum is recreated acoustically by a low sustained bass-clarinet air tone and a barely audible sanxian body resonance. Marcus enters with a slow, spacious bass phrase, playing only five notes across two bars. Min uses kick, rim, and brushed handclap rather than full snare.

Wenyi’s part begins with single low notes allowed to decay fully. Each phrase uses a different Slide Pickup distance: first a semitone, then a whole tone, then a narrow minor-third scoop that nearly becomes a glissando but stops before sounding theatrical. The middle section removes drums entirely for eight bars while Marcus and Wenyi trade low-register activity. Carla changes harmony only twice, allowing fretless inflection to carry most of the emotional movement.

The final minute restores the full groove quietly. No climax arrives. Wenyi plays one final Skin-Backbeat exactly with the last clap, then lets the membrane resonance fade while the polysynth chord remains for several seconds. The EP ends with the imagined sound of the turntable motor being switched off.

